name: reachability
boss: Portbreaker
flavor: I count the doors before you knock.
brief: Four questions, in order, when something will not connect: is the link
brief: up, does it have an address, is there a route, does dns work. Answer
brief: them in that order and you will almost never guess.
brief:
brief: ping proves a host answers. traceroute shows where the packets stop.
brief: netstat shows what your own machine is doing about it.
---
q: {does @ip@ even answer -- send three pings and stop|ping @ip@ three times, then stop|send exactly three pings to @ip@}
a: ping -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -c
hint: -c sets how many pings to send before stopping
---
q: {just need loss percent -- ping @ip@ quietly, summary only|ping @ip@ with per-packet output suppressed, summary only|ping @ip@ and print only the final summary}
a: ping -c 3 -q @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -q
hint: -q suppresses per-packet lines, printing only the final summary
---
q: {don't flood the link -- ping @ip@ one second apart|ping @ip@, spacing packets one second apart|space out the pings to @ip@ by one second each}
a: ping -i 1 -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -i
hint: -i sets the interval between packets, in seconds
---
q: {two interfaces, test one specifically -- ping @ip@ out of @iface@|ping @ip@ through @iface@|send the pings to @ip@ out of @iface@}
a: ping -I @iface@ -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -I
hint: -I picks which interface the pings go out of
---
q: {probe for MTU trouble -- ping @ip@ with a 1000 byte payload|ping @ip@ with a 1000 byte payload, to probe for MTU trouble|send 1000 byte pings to @ip@ to test for MTU issues}
a: ping -s 1000 -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -s
hint: -s sets the payload size in bytes, useful for MTU testing
---
q: {@ip@ may be dead -- give the ping run up after 5 seconds|cap the whole ping run to @ip@ at 5 seconds|stop pinging @ip@ after 5 seconds, whatever happens}
a: ping -w 5 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -w
hint: -w caps the whole run's wall-clock time, in seconds
---
q: {ping @ip@, waiting at most 2 seconds for each reply|cap the wait for each reply from @ip@ at 2 seconds|ping @ip@, waiting no more than 2 seconds per reply}
a: ping -W 2 -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -W
hint: -W caps how long to wait for each individual reply
---
q: {is @ip@ on the local segment -- ping with ttl fixed at 5 hops|ping @ip@ with the ttl fixed at 5 hops|send pings to @ip@ that expire after 5 hops}
a: ping -t 5 -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -t
hint: -t sets the ip ttl, capping how many hops a packet can cross
---
q: {the link may corrupt bits -- ping @ip@ with the pattern ff|ping @ip@ with ff as the byte pattern filling the payload|fill the ping payload to @ip@ with the pattern ff}
a: ping -p ff -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -p
hint: -p fills the payload with a repeating hex pattern you choose
---
q: {need samples fast -- ping @ip@ as soon as each reply lands|ping @ip@ adaptively, firing as soon as a reply lands|speed up pings to @ip@, sending as fast as replies allow}
a: ping -A -c 3 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: ping
teach: ping -A
hint: -A sends the next ping as soon as the last reply arrives, instead of waiting
---
q: pings aren't getting through -- %show% the path to @ip@
a: traceroute @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute
hint: traceroute with no flags shows the path packets take, hop by hop
---
q: {no dns wait per hop -- trace to @ip@ without resolving names|trace to @ip@ skipping name resolution for speed|run the trace to @ip@ fast, with no hostname lookups}
a: traceroute -n @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -n
hint: -n skips resolving each hop's hostname, so it runs faster
---
q: {the trace to @ip@ is dragging on -- cap it at 10 hops|cap the trace to @ip@ at 10 hops|trace to @ip@ but stop after 10 hops}
a: traceroute -m 10 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -m
hint: -m caps the maximum number of hops traced
---
q: {three probes per hop is too slow -- trace @ip@ with one|trace to @ip@ sending just one probe per hop|cut the probes per hop to @ip@ to one, not the default three}
a: traceroute -q 1 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -q
hint: -q sets how many probes are sent per hop, default is three
---
q: {start the trace to @ip@ at hop 5, skipping the first four|trace to @ip@ starting from hop 5|skip the first four hops tracing to @ip@, start at hop 5}
a: traceroute -f 5 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -f
hint: -f sets the starting ttl, skipping the earlier hops
---
q: {a firewall may drop udp -- trace @ip@ with icmp echo|trace to @ip@ with icmp echo probes instead of udp|switch the trace to @ip@ over to icmp echo}
a: traceroute -I @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -I
hint: -I switches the probe type to icmp echo instead of udp
---
q: {trace to @ip@ out of @iface@|trace to @ip@ through @iface@|send the trace to @ip@ out of @iface@}
a: traceroute -i @iface@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -i
hint: -i picks which interface the probes go out of
---
q: {trace to @ip@ using @ip2@ as a chosen source address|trace to @ip@ with @ip2@ as the source address you pick|set @ip2@ as the source address for a trace to @ip@}
a: traceroute -s @ip2@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -s
hint: -s sets the source address the probes claim to be from
---
q: {firewall may treat this port differently -- trace @ip@, port @port@|trace to @ip@ targeting port @port@|aim the trace to @ip@ at destination port @port@}
a: traceroute -p @port@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -p
hint: -p sets the destination port the probes target
---
q: {trace to @ip@, waiting at most 2 seconds for each probe|cap each probe's wait time at 2 seconds while tracing to @ip@|trace to @ip@, waiting no more than 2 seconds per probe}
a: traceroute -w 2 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -w
hint: -w caps how long to wait for each probe's reply
---
q: {trace to @ip@ showing every packet received|trace to @ip@ verbosely, printing every packet received|run a verbose trace to @ip@, showing every packet}
a: traceroute -v @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -v
hint: -v prints every packet received, not just the summary
---
q: {trace to @ip@ bypassing the normal routing tables|trace to @ip@, skipping the normal routing tables|send trace probes to @ip@ direct, bypassing routing}
a: traceroute -r @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -r
hint: -r sends probes directly, bypassing the normal routing tables
---
q: {@ip@ is rate-limiting you -- pause half a second between probes|trace to @ip@ with half a second between probes|space the probes to @ip@ half a second apart}
a: traceroute -z 500 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -z
hint: -z pauses between probes, in milliseconds
---
q: {trace to @ip@ with the first probe's ttl set to 1|set the first probe's ttl to 1, tracing to @ip@|give the very first probe to @ip@ a ttl of 1}
a: traceroute -t 1 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -t
hint: -t sets the ttl of the very first probe
---
q: {trace to @ip@ with 60 byte probe packets|set the probe packet length to 60, tracing to @ip@|make each probe packet to @ip@ 60 bytes long}
a: traceroute -l 60 @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -l
hint: -l sets the length of the probe packets themselves
---
q: {fragmentation may break the path to @ip@ -- forbid it on the probes|trace to @ip@ with fragmentation disabled|forbid the trace packets to @ip@ from being fragmented}
a: traceroute -F @ip@
why: live-net
ref: traceroute
teach: traceroute -F
hint: -F sets the don't-fragment bit on the trace packets
---
q: {site loads by ip but not by name -- look up @host@'s address|resolve @host@ to its address|find the address @host@ resolves to}
a: nslookup @host@
why: live-net
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
hint: nslookup with just a name asks your configured resolver
---
q: {resolver's broken -- look up @host@ via @ip@ as dns server|resolve @host@ using @ip@ as the dns server you name|query @ip@ directly for @host@}
a: nslookup @host@ @ip@
why: live-net
ref: nslookup
teach: nslookup
hint: naming a server after the host queries it directly, bypassing your resolver
---
q: {see what your machine is doing on the network -- what sockets is it using|report the sockets this machine is currently using|list this machine's current sockets}
a: netstat
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat
hint: netstat alone lists the sockets currently in use
---
q: check what's accepting connections -- %show% only listening sockets
a: netstat -l
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -l
hint: -l filters down to sockets in the listening state
---
q: %show% every socket, listening or not
a: netstat -a
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -a
hint: -a shows every socket, listening and connected alike
---
q: dns is down -- %show% sockets numerically, no names or ports resolved
a: netstat -n
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -n
hint: -n skips resolving hostnames and service names to numbers
---
q: %show% only tcp sockets
a: netstat -t
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
hint: -t filters to tcp sockets only
---
q: %show% only udp sockets
a: netstat -u
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -u
hint: -u filters to udp sockets only
---
q: which process has a port open -- %show% who owns each socket
a: netstat -p
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -p
hint: -p adds the pid and process name that owns each socket
---
q: %show% every listening tcp socket numerically, with its process
a: netstat -tlnp
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -t
hint: single-letter flags combine -- tlnp is t, l, n and p together
---
q: %show% the routing table using netstat
a: netstat -r
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -r
hint: -r switches netstat to print the routing table instead of sockets
---
q: %show% unix domain sockets
a: netstat -x
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -x
hint: -x filters to unix domain sockets
---
q: %show% raw sockets
a: netstat -w
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -w
hint: -w filters to raw sockets
---
q: %show% netstat output with extended information
a: netstat -e
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -e
hint: -e adds extended fields like user and inode
---
q: ipv6 addresses keep getting cut off -- %show% netstat with wide columns
a: netstat -W
why: live-net
ref: netstat
teach: netstat -W
hint: -W widens columns so long addresses print in full
---
q: {ping the ipv6 loopback ::1 three times|send three pings to the ipv6 loopback ::1|ping ::1, the ipv6 loopback, stopping after three}
a: ping6 -c 3 ::1
why: live-net
ref: ping6
teach: ping6
hint: the whole world is moving to ipv6; the tools have a 6 on the end
---
q: {trace the ipv6 path to ::1|trace the path to the ipv6 loopback ::1|show the ipv6 path a trace to ::1 takes}
a: traceroute6 ::1
why: live-net
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6
hint: traceroute6 is traceroute's ipv6-only counterpart
---
q: {trace the ipv6 path to ::1 without resolving names|trace to ::1 over ipv6, skipping name resolution|trace the ipv6 path to ::1 with no hostname lookups}
a: traceroute6 -n ::1
why: live-net
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -n
hint: -n skips name resolution here too
---
q: {trace the ipv6 path to ::1 no further than 10 hops|cap the ipv6 trace to ::1 at 10 hops|trace over ipv6 to ::1, stopping after 10 hops}
a: traceroute6 -m 10 ::1
why: live-net
ref: traceroute6
teach: traceroute6 -m
hint: -m caps hops the same way it does for traceroute
